yle glowing down at the city below  him  is one of the dark knight's most iconic  poses and  the snyder cut recreates it in stunning  fashion  as you may have noticed however that's  not just any gargoyle  wearing a heavy hood with bony hands and  a skull-like face  the statue resembles the grim reaper  more than a traditional winged beast  even for gotham city it's pretty  hardcore  as it turns out it's also a comic book  reference the same gargoyle appeared on  the cover of detective comics number 682  which was illustrated by graham nolan a  long time batman artist who also  co-created the masked villain bane  as far as story is concerned detective  comics number 682 doesn't have much to  do with the snyder cut writer chuck  dixon's little yarn revolves around the  b-list batman villain kg beast and a  nuclear device  neither of which feature injustice  league although kg beast  does make a brief appearance in batman  vs superman more than anything snyder  probably just thought detective comics  number 682 had a cool cover  and hey he wasn't wrong the stone family  is made up almost entirely of  super geniuses so it shouldn't come as  too much of a surprise that they're  quite well read too  still there's a big difference between  just liking a book and  actually hanging a poster of it in your  home and yet  that's exactly what victor and his  father silas have done  as cyborg broods in his gotham city  apartment you can catch a poster  referencing friends kafka's classic 1915  novella the metamorphosis  on his wall if you've read kafka's story  the thematic parallels should be obvious  in the metamorphosis a young salesman  named gregor samsa  wakes up one day and discovers that he's  been turned into a giant insect  then struggles to adapt to his new  situation as his humanity  slowly slips away for cyborg who's  grappling with who  and what he is now that he's half  machine  it's a tale that probably resonates kind  of heavily at least cyborg gets a  happier ending  while victor ultimately reclaims his  identity and finds a new home with the  justice league  gregor starves to death after becoming a  burden on his family  the extended epilogue that caps off zack  snyder's justice league gives fans their  longest and best look at what the  director had planned for the nightmare  storyline first introduced in batman vs  superman  she was my world  and you took her from me it's not the  only taste we get of the dceu's  post-apocalyptic future though when  cyborg grabs the mother box in star labs  he catches a glimpse of the horrors in  store for earth's heroes  and as you've probably guessed that  glimpse comes with a few  easter eggs too the biggest one is the  presence of another green lantern  albeit one who has fallen to evil  superman's attacks  while he doesn't wear a domino mask in  the comics the lantern lying in the  ruins of the hall of justice is almost  certainly kilawog  one of the most popular members of the  green lantern corps  kilowog who hails from sector 674 is  traditionally depicted as one of the  lanterns who train new corps members  there are  other references in this scene too the  hall of justice itself has been one of  the justice league's most popular  headquarters since 1973  when it made its debut in the animated  show's super friends in addition the  scene in which darkseid kills lois and  turns superman evil looks like it takes  place in the bat cave's ruins  look carefully and you'll see batman's  signature red screens  and his memorial to his late partner  robin among the wreckage  looks like clark might have good reason  to be mad at bruce after  all batman vs superman and zack snyder's  justice league  borrow a few key notes from the seminal  90s storyline  the death and return of superman as in  the comic books batman vs superman sees  a genetically engineered superweapon  called doomsday kill the man of steel  also like the comics when kal-el returns  he wears a black suit decorated with 